#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577} :activity/id #uuid "37fac92b-9502-52a2-8069-399fccbd8a62" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"} :step/id #uuid "a6ab275a-61df-5648-811a-176fda344fe5" :step/math-academy-id 30861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360571}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"} :step/id #uuid "8627eaba-93e3-51d5-b350-72ee5af3e635" :step/math-academy-id 30862 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "60b2ea1e-4b9a-5557-99ae-a83016e785a6" :step/math-academy-id 30863 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "26ddca41-ae16-5395-ae11-41c4d70303c6" :step/math-academy-id 30864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168061 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for single variable functions $f=f(x)$ and $x=x(t)$ states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that $f = f(x,y),$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$ are single-variable functions. How do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d t}$ in this case?\n\nThe functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are known as **intermediate variables.** They are independent variables for the function $f(x,y),$ but are themselves dependent on the variable $t.$\n\nOne way to consider how $f(x,y)$ depends on $t$ is to draw a tree diagram.\n\nStarting with $f(x,y),$ we draw a branch for each intermediate variable. Then, we draw a branch for both $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ which are both dependent on the variable $t.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdc47e038ecaba62ae612a0ac0bc07067af232353fc4fc169bf4be57b3abbd1f.png)\n\nWe then label each branch with the derivative describing how the function changes due to a change in the variable. \n\n![](images/ec/ece6012e358b5b6920395df88acd8d7037a7b15bed1add1220365ba72bd24cbd.png)\n\nThen, traversing each branch, we compute the product of the derivatives along each branch and place the result at the end of the branch.\n\n![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nFinally, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we sum the results for each branch:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}\n = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThis result is called the **multivariable chain rule**." :tutorial/id #uuid "80ad8a26-7d43-52d6-b0ba-e1d04283e5ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "03fe551f-81c6-5c42-9baf-d6d12895ab47" :step/math-academy-id 30893 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168062 :tutorial/content "How do we apply the multivariable chain rule if the intermediate variables are also dependent on two variables? \n\nFor example, suppose we have\n\n$$\nf = f(x,y), \\qquad x=g(s,t), \\qquad y=h(s,t).\n$$\n\nHow do we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}?$ \n\nFirst, let's construct a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/ca/ca524eda2e6be3354fcb72bed52fcaf22257073c3228d74850f8226f9ef0af6f.png)\n\nNow, since we're interested in $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s},$ we highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $s:$\n\n![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nIf we keep $t$ fixed and differentiate $f$ with respect to $s,$ then according to the chain rule for two intermediate variables and one independent variable, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we wanted to compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t},$ we would highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nKeeping $s$ fixed and differentiating $f$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58d189b1-31fb-5b1b-bc4c-cff6be5e2f7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4958 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "2ffafece-1c0b-50f7-a3b1-e1b2d1bada36" :step/math-academy-id 30894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168063 :tutorial/content "We can generalize the chain rule to a multivariable function containing an arbitrary number of intermediate and independent variables.\n\nConsider the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf = f(x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_m)\n$$\n\nwith $m$ independent variables and, for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, n\\},$ the variable \n\n$$\nx_i = g_i(t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_n)\n$$\n\nis a differentiable function of $n$ independent variables. Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t_j} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_1}{\\partial t_j} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_2}{\\partial t_j} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_m}{\\partial t_j}\n$$\n\nfor $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots, n\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba7d2a4-05a6-5e62-8035-2610239633c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4959 :tutorial/title "The Generalized Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "9731a04f-c2d1-5342-b940-0313b71ca1eb" :step/math-academy-id 30895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168088 :tutorial/content "There is a special case of the multivariable chain rule when $f=f(x,y)$ and $y=g(x).$ \n\nNotice that $x$ is an independent variable here. Therefore, the chain rule becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}}_{⏟}}_{1}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44effd03-02e7-5eb1-8732-ee1522afa147" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5184 :tutorial/title "A Special Case of the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fdc61eff-9eab-552e-9092-f4040bdff21a" :step/math-academy-id 32539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166978}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420998} :activity/id #uuid "844676a2-0a90-5f03-a7c1-22515b371e49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a19a158-cb30-5c9e-9cf9-891ab114ad36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(2t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355012} {:answer/id #uuid "392f1f1c-1dad-5e72-b1e4-40cf201a767c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}t(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355179} {:answer/id #uuid "869f5f73-0c65-5003-ade6-8eeef3e8b4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355855} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0fe58-16c4-5d79-ab11-4893e9ff089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(s-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356369} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ace28-41b6-5a2e-8b64-9dee698d22fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314b903-87e4-5c8c-a8db-703d520d008b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1e3cf4a-155f-587d-ba03-cc737759407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94448" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cf7698e-82a3-5979-93cb-d2edffbb99d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9a8991-24fd-50f9-8941-a329e3133c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+10{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321230} {:answer/id #uuid "84b790fb-dce2-5ea5-9fbd-fb66967b78c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-20s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322195} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f027a-f67b-5d1f-b407-9ee3b85b3667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322598} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} {:answer/id #uuid "f675ee7a-8c5d-5a9a-90cb-d0e4dd971fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-15s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1de9ae-38d9-5d66-a275-9fc76c319cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd5b5db6-0961-5b1c-8f50-24c2f4348bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37132" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a9db776-142d-5ace-9b62-834d4e4b6a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021a846a-8825-55bf-a7b3-1dc5c5c5ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281709} {:answer/id #uuid "49b2d0a3-8640-5928-a7d2-f8a14102a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a1e1c7-9e8a-5f17-9500-4d7507d44b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283715} {:answer/id #uuid "efd819c9-60d2-5826-9266-3dba4a49aaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283779} {:answer/id #uuid "f5de75cd-c44e-5baf-9f07-d98f3333edab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} :answer-field/id #uuid "d658fcd0-7d37-5a87-a5b6-42706ffef583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdba7da9-8893-51f6-a38d-eccc847b176c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170670" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ef0c797-03b2-58eb-8a37-83236f476fe8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be18679-efe0-59e6-80ac-db92f8d96a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9edd72-2523-57c6-9665-681f1ed475c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358439} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcdf56c-f98e-5632-8b37-69db930425d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b3c97-4572-526c-8afd-d0a1d5757af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359141} {:answer/id #uuid "b33efd16-e5a4-5396-8988-6df4afae8ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f643ac2-8342-50cb-9136-e026a1d69059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfb1f74a-0323-5d9e-a2f6-877356ed98f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95118" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7bfbd495-1860-54a3-8d60-35f838cfca25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17573a89-2ada-5cfd-8592-1b89b18d61f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420990} {:answer/id #uuid "3856cad9-d2c1-55a4-adf6-784ae35a6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420991} {:answer/id #uuid "5c54b913-d15a-53bc-b2c6-f71477530f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420992} {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} {:answer/id #uuid "d87585d5-b5cd-5fbc-b58c-a3d2c45f4e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f118ee24-10a3-5bda-8238-4be0365cace0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f22c7a35-af74-59c8-b71b-cae8347ea97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37206" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+{z}^{2},$ where $x={s}^{2}+{t}^{2},$ $y=\\frac{s}{t}$ and $z=s{t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+{c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}s{t}^{4},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{s}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{s}{t})\\cdot 2s+({s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2})\\cdot {t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{t}+\\frac{{s}^{2}}{t}+t+st+st+2s{t}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+t+2st+2s{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=1+2+2=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "926bef20-92d0-5cd4-928e-9a8dd40763b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420997}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420989}]}
